The rules restate, in their comments and messages, the advice they enforce,
and each rule links to the page it comes from. Source and terms:

  - Plain Language Action and Information Network, Federal Plain Language
    Guidelines, published at plainlanguage.gov and now archived at
    https://github.com/GSA/plainlanguage.gov/tree/main/_pages/guidelines.
    A work of the United States government, in the public domain in the
    United States, and dedicated worldwide under CC0 1.0 Universal
    (https://github.com/GSA/plainlanguage.gov/blob/main/LICENSE.md).

    SimpleWords, Strict.Words, and Omit transcribe the list in "Use simple
    words and phrases", with each verb given in its four forms. Legalese
    takes Joseph Kimble's list as quoted in "Avoid jargon", with the legal
    entries of the simple-words list; Jargon takes that page's list of
    filler phrases. Wordy takes the phrases and doublets in "Be concise",
    and HiddenVerbs and DoubleNegatives start from the examples in "Avoid
    hidden verbs" and "Use positive language". The 150-word paragraph limit
    is from "Write short paragraphs".

The guidelines quote and credit published authors, among them Joseph
Kimble, Bryan A. Garner, and Rudolf Flesch; rule comments that quote the
guidelines quote those passages as the guidelines give them. This package is
not endorsed by or affiliated with the United States government, the General
Services Administration, or the Plain Language Action and Information
Network.
